## 1. Introduction
Among all kinds of malignant tumors, breast cancer (BC) is one of the most common malignancies and it is the fifth most common cause of cancer death in women (Harbeck et al., 2019). The morbidity of patients is about $25\mathrm{-}30\%$ and the mortality is about $14.7\%$ . There were an estimated 266,000 new cases of breast cancer in the United States with about 41,000 death cases (Harbeck et al., 2019; Miao et al., 2021), while the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC) reported half a million deaths from BC worldwide in 2012 (Ferlay et al., 2015). Therefore, it is urgent to detect and treat BC, and the carbohydrate CA15-3, a product of mucin 1 gene family, is one of the significant serum biomarkers for the clinical diagnosis of BC (Darwish et al., 2012). Elevated serum levels of the protein CA15-3 are applied to predict recurrences in patients and provide an additional target for treatment in advanced BC patients (Ribeiro et al., 2021). Consequently, it is necessary to develop a sensitive and specific detection of method for CA15-3, which is beneficial to the diagnosis of BC and reduce the mortality of cancer patients. At present, the strategies for commonly determining CA15-3 are listed as electrochemiluminescence (ECL) (Jiang et al., 2015; Ke et al., 2018), enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) (Li et al., 2010), fluorescent immunoassay (FL) (Wu et al., 2021) and so forth. Since these strategies are imperfect owing to intricate operation, photochemical (PEC) biosensors are a rapidly developing analytical method that benefits from their advantages such as the background signal negligible, easy of miniaturization and inexpensive (Hou et al., 2016).
In the past several years, PEC biosensing technology has rapidly been establishing into one of the most attractive research orientations in the field of analytical technology and the overall performance rest on the choice of materials in biosensing. While the heterojunction composed of hollow structure materials has attracted widespread attention in many new energy resources such as solar cells (Qadir et al., 2016), photo­ catalytic degradation (Wang et al., 2019) and PEC biosensing (Zhang et al., 2019). Rationally design of hollow nanomaterials are formed new heterojunctions will tremendously improve the photochemical proper­ ties and great significance for abounding present technologies (Shang et al., 2020). (ⅰ) Multi-shell hollow nanostructures benefit from the higher weight fraction of their active substances and generally increase the energy density of semiconductor heterojunction materials (Yu et al., 2016). (ⅱ) Hollow nanomaterials boost the efficiency of light collection by their internal voids for multiple reflections, increasing photoexcited charge carriers (Peng et al., 2021). (ⅲ) Due to the action of the electric field built into the hollow nanomaterial, the separation of the photo­ excited charge can be accelerated at the interface of the heterojunction, and the photostability of composite photocatalysts are ameliorated (Wang et al., 2019). Therefore, hollow nanomaterials are considered to provide a feasible way to be adopt as heterojunction materials for photochemical biosensing based on the excellent photoelectric chemical properties.
Remarkably, the combination of hollow semiconductor materials to constitute type-II heterojunction is an effective mean to enhance the performance of light capture. For instance, our group’s (Zhang et al., 2020) rationally designed hierarchical hollow $\mathrm{ZnCdS@MoS_{2}}$ hetero­ structured cages with efficient separation of photogenerated carriers, which are benefited from the cross-band arrangement between the two semiconductors to spatially separate the photogenerated electron-hole pairs (Zhang et al., 2020). For a classical type-II heterojunction, the photogenerated electrons and the holes migrate freely and rapidly in the conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) of the composite semi­ conductor material, respectively (Low et al., 2017). It is advantageous to accelerate carrier migration and the improvement of photoelectric conversion efficiency. The metal sulfide $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ with two-dimensional layered structure, which has attracted attention, emerges an excellent ability to absorb visible light due to special structure and narrow band gap $(1.9\mathrm{~eV}-2.4\mathrm{~eV})$ (Ma et al., 2021). Nevertheless, the most of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ formed flower-like block structures for disordered self-assembly, which greatly reduced the active sites of materials and impaired its capacity to capture light as the surface material of heterojunction. This feature seriously restricts the application of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ as a heterojunction unit and PEC sensing.
Hence, in this paper, the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ hollow nanorods $\mathrm{(In_{2}S_{3}–H N R s)}$ with surrounded ultrathin nanosheets were synthesized by self-template hy­ drothermal method growing in direction. The synthesized the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}.$ HNRs demonstrated superior photoelectrochemical properties compared to the random self-assembled $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ nanoflowers materials. Simultaneously, compared with traditional $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ nanorods, the carefully-synthesized $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs can both possess the advantages of nanomaterials and maximize the performance of ultra-thin subunits, which enormously reduced the transmission distance of photogenerated carriers and weakened the electron-hole pair recombination to improve the photoelectric conversion efficiency (Yu et al., 2017). Further, to enrich the combination types of heterojunction materials and enhance the ability of composite materials to capture light, the ZnCdS hollow dodecahedral nanocages (ZnCdS-HDCs) were creatively coupled and assembled into a pristine double-hollow heterojunction ZnCdS-HDCs $@\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs. Eventually, an innovative PEC biosensor for detecting CA15-3 was established, which was based on ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-HNRs, and the as-prepared PEC biosensor was endowed distinguished sensitivity and ultra-low detection limit with exceptional detection property for CA15-3.
## 2. Experimental section
Chemicals and characterization have been described on Supporting information.
## 2.1. Syntheses of ZnCdS hollow dodecahedron cages (ZnCdS-HDCs)
ZnCdS-HDCs with ZIF-8 as precursor were prepared according to our group’s previous method with partially optimization (Zhang et al., 2020). 4 mmol of Zinc nitrate hexahydrate and 8 mmol 2-methylimida­ zole were dissolved in $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ methanol, respectively. Then, the above two solutions were blended under magnetic stir for $3\ \mathrm{min}$ and the mixture was incubated for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ without interruption at room tempera­ ture. Afterward, the white precipitate was washed three times with ethanol by filtration and dried under vacuum for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain ZIF-8 dodecahedron cages (ZIF-8-DCs). The as-obtained $200~\mathrm{~mg~}$ of ZIF-8-DCs were dispersed in ethanol containing tannic acid $\scriptstyle200{\mathrm{~mL}},$ 1 g). After stirring for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , the white precipitate was gathered by centrifugation, washed with ethanol three times and dried in a vacuum oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight. For preparing the ZnS hollow dodecahedron cages (ZnS-HDCs), $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of the yolk-shelled ZIF-8 (YS-ZIF-8) and 400 mg of thioacetamide (TAA) were dispersed in $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol with stirring, and a classical hydrothermal reaction was conducted at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The ZnS-HDCs were collected by centrifugation and washed with ethanol several times. The ZnS-HDCs powders were placed into 30 mL of ethanol containing cadmium chloride $(70~\mathrm{mg})$ . The yellow sus­ pension was obtained by cation-exchange reaction at $160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Eventually, the suspension was washed with ethanol by centrifugation and pursued by vacuum-drying at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2. Syntheses of MIL-68 using sodium acetate as competing reagent
$90\mathrm{mg}$ of Indium nitrate hydrate $(\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{xH}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ was dissolved in N, N-Dimethylformamide (DMF, $10\mathrm{mL}$ ) to tab solution A. Simultaneously, 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B D C_{\it{3}}}$ , $110{\mathrm{~mg}}.$ ) was add to $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF under magnetic stirring to form solution B (Wang et al., 2017). Subsequently, the solution B was tardily decanted into solution A with electromagnetic stirring. $25~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.05{\mathrm{~M~}}$ sodium acetate $\mathrm{(CH_{3}C O O N a)}$ aqueous solution was injected into the resultant solution via pipettor. Then, the obtained uniform solution was placed in oil bath at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After the temperature was cooled down to room temperature, the 0.05M-MIL-68 were collected after several rinse-centrifugation cy­ cles with ethanol and dried in a vacuum ( ${\bf\tilde{\boldsymbol{6}}}\boldsymbol{0}^{\mathrm{~o~}}{\bf\tilde{\Lambda}}$ , 12 h). As a contrast experiment, MIL-68 was prepared under the same experimental condi­ tions $25~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O N a}$ aqueous solution (0.5 M-MIL-68) and without $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O N a}$ aqueous solution (0 M-MIL-68).
## 2.3. Synthesis of $I n_{2}S_{3}$ hollow nanorods $(I n_{2}S_{3^{-}}H N R s)$
To synthesize the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ hollow nanorods $(\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s)$ , $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of asprepared 0.05 M-MIL-68 and $200~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of thiourea (TA) were scattered into anhydrous alcohol $(30~\mathrm{mL}$ ) under ultrasound for 5 min. Then, the suspension was transferred to a Teflon-lined stainless-steel autoclave $\mathrm{50mL}$ in capacity) and heated at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ . After the hydrothermal sulfidation reaction was completed and cooled to room temperature, the $0.05~\mathrm{M}\cdot$ - $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs suspension was centrifugally filtered and washed with ethanol for several times and dried overnight in a vacuum at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ For comparison, $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ nanoflowers $\mathrm{(In_{2}}S_{3}$ -NFs) were synthesized under the same reaction conditions without MIL-68. The 0 M-In2S3-HNRs and $0.5~\mathrm{M}\mathrm{-In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs were fabricated with 0 M-MIL-68 and 0.5 M-MIL-68 via the same strategy, respectively.
## 2.4. Fabrication of ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-HNRs electrodes
Initially, the indium tin oxide (ITO) slices ( $1\mathrm{cm}\times3\mathrm{cm}\times1\mathrm{cm})$ ) were treated with acetone, 1 M NaOH ethanol/water mixed solution $(\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v}$ ,
1:1) and distilled water by ultrasonic for $15\mathrm{min}$ , respectively. Secondly, the $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $5.0\mathrm{\mg\mL}^{-1}$ ZnCdS-HDCs slurry was smoothly dripped on the glossy ITO surface with an effective area of $0.0961\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ and heated at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . Finally, the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s$ aqueous suspension $(6\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $3.0\mathrm{mg}$ $\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L}^{-1}\mathbf{\bar{\Phi}}.$ ) was dropped onto the as-prepared ZnCdS-HDCs electrode and dried in the oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ to gain the ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-HNRs electrodes. As a comparison of experiment, the ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-NFs heterojunction electrode was prepared with the same ratio and method.
## 2.5. Fabrication of photoelectrochemical sensor
In brief, ${8\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of chitosan solution (CS, $0.5\%$ was modified onto the pretreated $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}}S_{3}\mathrm{-}$ -HNRs electrodes and hatching for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ under room temperature in order to provide the sufficient amino groups. Following, $8\upmu\mathrm{L}2.5\%$ glutaraldehyde (GA) solution was dropped onto asprepared electrode surface and dried at room temperature for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Through the aldimine condensation reaction, the amino group in the chitosan film binds with the aldehyde group abundant in glutaraldehyde for coupling the antibody. Then, the excess glutaraldehyde solution was removed by PBS solution after the groups were successfully combined, and $40\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ of the anti-CA15-3 $(6\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added onto the surface of the obtained electrode to incubate at $20^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{h}$ . After the completion of incubation, the anti-CA15-3 physically adsorbed on the electrode was eliminated by $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS $\mathrm{(pH}7.4)$ solution, and then $1\%$ bovine serum albumin (BSA,dissolved in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS) was added onto the modified electrode for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ to block the inactive site and the exceed BSA was aspirated with $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ PBS $\mathrm{(pH~7.4)}$ solution. Finally, the as-prepared electrode was incubated at room temperature with different concen­ tration gradients of CA15-3 for $80~\mathrm{{min}}$ and wiped off PBS to limit nonspecifically adsorbed CA15-3. The construction procedure of the PEC biosensor was revealed in Scheme 1B.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Structure characterization of $I n_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs and ZnCdS-HDCs
The preparation process of ZnCdS with hollow dodecahedral cages by metal cation-exchange strategy was revealed in Scheme 1A. In a nutshell, the dodecahedral ZIF-8 is synthesized via the self-assembly method and etching with a tannic acid to obtain the yolk-shelled ZIF-8 (YS-ZIF-8) (Zhang et al., 2018). As shown in Fig. 1A and Fig. S1A, the morphologies of the ZIF-8 and YS-ZIF-8 were characterized by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) analysis. The ZIF-8 and YS-ZIF-8 exhibit a representative rhombic dodecahedron feature with a particulate diameter of approximately $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Through the X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern, the obtained ZIF-8 and YS-ZIF-8 still preserve the same crystal structure, which are indicated that no phase transition or new manufacture formation occurs during the chemical etching (Fig. S1B). Afterward, the YS-ZIF-8 as the precursor, $s^{2-}$ was released from TAA to react with $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ on the surface of the precursor forming the ZnS-HDCs by a hydrothermal process. It is a remarkable fact that ZnS polyhedral cages (Fig. 1B) display conspicuous hollow dodecahedral structure, indicating that the structure of the precursor was well inherited. For the purpose of preparing the ZnCdS-HDCs, a cation-exchange method is adopted, the structural alteration of ZnS-HDCs to the ternary sulfide ZnCdS-HDCs is induced. The FESEM (Fig. 1C) images clearly disclose the distinctive hollow dodecahedral structure with a particulate diameter of approximately $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ . As illustrated by the PXRD pattern (Fig. S1C), the characteristic peaks at $24.8^{\circ}$ , $26.5^{\circ}$ , $28.2^{\circ}$ , $36.7^{\circ}$ , $43.7^{\circ}$ , $47.9^{\circ}$ , and $51.9^{\circ}$ observed match well with the standard CdS (JCPDS, No. 77–2306), whereas (002), (110), and (112) peaks of $z_{\mathrm{n}S}$ (JCPDS No. 75-1547) were found at $28.4^{\circ}$ , 47.4◦, and $56.2^{\circ}$ (Zhang et al., 2020), respectively.
To prepare $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}–\mathrm{HNRs}$ , a hydrothermal sulfide reaction was carried out by adding thiourea (TA) to MIL-68 as self-template. The overall synthetic processes of hollow $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ NRs were declared in Scheme 1C.
The conventional MIL-68 shows uniform hexagonal prisms with the average length of $8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and the diameter of about $500\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 1D). The hexagonal MIL-68 prisms are formed by the decomposition of the DMF under solvothermal conditions to produce dimethylamine, which acts as a Brønsted base, leading to the deprotonation of $\mathrm{{H_{2}B D C}}$ , and eventually to the formation of metal-BDC composite material (Guo et al., 2012; Hermes et al., 2007). In this paper, the size of the self-template was innovatively changed by adding salt solution (sodium acetate) in the process of metal and organic ligand binding to compete with it. As shown in Fig. 1E, the length of the modified 0.05M-MIL-68-NRs are approximately $2\mathrm{-}3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and these MIL-68 had the same characteristic peak, indicating that the addition of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O N a}$ did not cause the phase transformation of MIL-68 to produce new products (Fig. S1D). Then, as-synthesized MIL-68-NRs are transformed into the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ -HNRs through a hydrothermal sulfuration at $160^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{9\mathrm{h}}$ . The unique hollow construction of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ nanorods possessed the length of $2\mathrm{-}3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and the closer observation emerged around shell the ultra-thin $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ nanosheets were evenly distributed inside and outside (Fig. 1F). To further explore the internal morphology of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s$ , which were examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM) in Fig. 2. The TEM images (Fig. 2A and B) definitely reveal the hollow $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ nanorod structure and ultra-thin nanosheets $(\sim10\ \mathrm{nm})$ , respectively. By high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) lattice image and selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern (Fig. 2C), the lattice spacing of about $0.325\mathrm{nm}$ corresponded to the (311) crystal plane of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s$ , and the well-crystallized and polycrystalline properties of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs were confirmed. Mean­ while, the elemental mapping images (Fig. 2D–F) demonstrate that In and S elements are consistently distributed the shell of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ -HNRs.
Subsequently, PXRD used to explore the phase transition of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ - HNRs with the different MIL-68 precursors as self-templates. It can be observed from Fig. 3A that the different templates of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ -HNRs possess three normal peaks at $27.4^{\circ}$ , $33.23^{\circ}$ and $47.7^{\circ}$ , corresponding to (311), (400) and (440) crystal planes of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ (JCPDS, No. 65-0459) (Hou et al., 2020; Liu et al., 2021), respectively.
To further investigate the composition and chemical valence of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs and ZnCdS-HDCs, we performed X-ray photoelectron spec­ troscopy (XPS) measurement on the samples. In Fig. 3B and Fig. S2A, In, Zn, Cd, and S can be verified by the survey spectrum. Then, the highresolution XPS spectrum of In 3d manifested two characteristic peaks at 452.1 eV for In $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and $444.6\mathrm{eV}$ for In $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ corresponding to $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ (Fig. 3C). The Fig. 3D shows an asymmetric peak, which can be divided into two peaks at $162.5\mathrm{eV}$ and $161.2\mathrm{eV}$ , proving the existence of $S^{2-}$ in the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (Ma et al., 2021). Besides, the chemical valence states of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{C d}^{3+}$ were also confirmed by the high-resolution XPS spectra of $\mathtt{Z n2p}$ and Cd 3d. The two peaks of 1045.1 eV $(\mathrm{Zn}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})$ and 1023.3 eV $(\mathrm{Zn}2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})$ indicated the presence of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , while the peaks at 416.4 eV and $409.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ were attributed to Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ of $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ (Fig. S2B and Fig. S2C) (Chava et al., 2019). For $\mathtt{s2p}$ , the two peaks were located at 163.5 eV $(\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}_{1/2})$ and 161.9 eV $\l(S2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})$ that is consistent with the values of $\displaystyle{s^{2-}}$ (Fig. S2D). Therefore, the above analysis conse­ quences indicate that the successful preparation of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs and ZnCdS-HDCs.
The photoelectric properties of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNRs}$ with different morphol­ ogies were examined by PEC of as-prepared products with 0.1 M AA as a sacrifice agent in Fig. 3E. We can clearly comprehend that the photo­ electric properties of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -NFs (curve d, $0.25~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ formed by selfassembly without template are significantly lower than those of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}.$ - HNRs synthesized by self-template. Furthermore, photocurrent perfor­ mance of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -NFs is only 1/24 of that of the $0.05\mathrm{M}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (curve c, $6.05~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ . Due to the unique hollow nanostructures of the thin permeable shell can shorten the charge diffusion path, which facilitates separation and transfer of photogenerated electron-hole pairs. Mean­ while, the inner and outer surfaces to place locally incompatible co­ factors of oxidative and reductive reactions benefit from their special shell structure, and the reflection and scattering of multiple light in the cavity enhance the light absorption of the hollow structure.
Nevertheless, the photocurrent properties of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ -HNRs obtained from the template are also different. The transient photocurrent of $0.05~\mathrm{M}\cdot$ $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{HNRs}$ with closer to nanometer size and uniform distributed ultrathin nanosheets are significantly superior than $0\mathrm{M}–\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (curve a, $1.02\upmu\mathrm{A})$ and $0.5\mathrm{M}\mathrm{-In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (curve b, $1.7\upmu\mathrm{A}_{\cdot}^{\cdot}$ . This phenomenon was also verified in the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) test species using $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ as the redox probe with an electron trans­ port resistance equal to the impedance of the semicircle diameter (Fan et al., 2016). The $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s$ exhibits lower electron-transfer imped­ ance $\mathrm{(R_{et})}$ than $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -NFs (curve d, $90.53\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ under light conditions, as shown in Fig. 3F. Similarly, in the absence of light, the impedance of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ -NFs (curve d, $103.27\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ is significantly greater than that of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs in Fig. S4A. It can be ascribed to the ultrathin nanosheet and peculiar hollow nanorods structure of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ , which are beneficial to boosting separation of light-induced charges by shortening diffusion distance from bulk to surface. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{R_{et}}$ value of 0.05 $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs was about $33.86\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ , which is better than the 0 $\mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (curve c, $58.58\Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and $0.5\ \mathrm{M}{\cdot}\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (curve b, $52.83\ \Omega\ \mathrm{cm}^{2})$ ,and the results are demonstrated the analogous without illumination in Fig. S4A. All the above-obtained results of electro­ chemical and photoelectrochemical explorations expound that the hol­ low reasonable-size nanorods and uniform the ultrathin nanosheet of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ promotes its photoelectric nature.
## 3.2. Photoelectrochemical characterization of ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3- HNRs double-hollow composite electrodes
To elucidate the mechanism of the hollow composites enhanced the photoelectric properties, the UV–Vis diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (DRS) of the as-prepared photoactive materials were conducted. It can be observed from Fig. 4 A that ZnCdS-HDCs and $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs exhibits an obvious absorption edge around $530\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $570\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Nevertheless, the meticulous design double hollow heterojunction demonstrates stronger light-absorption in the visible region (about 600 nm). The band composition of the unique heterojunction was charac­ terized by the Tauc’s plots and XPS valence band spectrum $(\mathrm{E_{VB}})$ . From the empirical equation, the band gaps $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{Eg}})$ of $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNR}s$ and ZnCdSHDCs were calculated to be $1.98\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $2.28\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , severally (Fig. 4B).
As depicted in Fig. 4C and Fig. S4B, the XPS valence band spectrum was emerged to verify $\bf{E}_{\mathrm{{VB}}}$ of the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs (1.41 eV) and ZnCdS-HDCs $(2.16~\mathrm{eV})$ . Hence, according to the formula $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{CB}}=\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{VB}}-\mathrm{E}\mathrm{g}$ (Liu et al., 2017), the conduction band $(\mathrm{E_{CB}})$ reckoned about $-0.57$ eV for $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}–\mathrm{HNRs}$ and $-0.12\ \mathrm{~eV}$ for ZnCdS-HDCs. Therefore, the hollow nanomaterials of ZnCdS-HDCs and $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{S_{3}}$ -HNRs effectively generate the original type II heterojunction on the electrode surface by their cross-matched bandgap structures to reduce the recombination of pho­ togenerated electron hole pairs. The mechanism of the ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-HNRs innovative heterojunction is schematically shown in Scheme 1D.
So as to probe insight into the enhancement mechanism of the energy-level matched $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ double-hollow heter­ ojunction, we performed a series of electrochemical tests to characterize the well-design electrode. First of all, the steady-state PL quenching of ZnCdS-HDCs ${\ @\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}}$ -HNRs is enormously abated comparing with $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}.$ - HNRs, which was confirmed that hollow photoactive composites of the heterojunction inhibited photoelectric charge recombination, acceler­ ated charge migration, and thus photoelectrochemical performance enhanced (Fig. 4D). Additionally, the prepared materials were confirmed the typical n-type semiconductor by the positive slope of M-S curve in Fig. S4C. The $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ double-hollow heter­ ojunction possessed more excellent electron-hole pair separation ability and inhibited their recombination compared with the single ZnCdSHDCs, which can be obviously seen in the Bode-Phase diagram. On the basis of the formula is raised in Equ. (1) (Chen et al., 2018):
$$
\tau_{e}=\frac{1}{2\pi f_{m a x}}
$$
where $\tau_{\mathrm{e}}$ and $\mathrm{f}_{\mathrm{max}}$ represent the electronic life and the characteristic peak frequency in Bode-Phase diagram, respectively. The calculated electron lifetime of the single ZnCdS-HDCs is $252.24\upmu s$ , while the welldesigned $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}}S_{3}$ -HNRs double-hollow heterojunction is $503.29\upmu s$ (Fig. 4E). The longer electron lifetime means that the electron transfer process is faster and more efficient at transferring photoelectric charges. The incident-photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) measurement of $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H I}$ NRs were further proved that the constructed hierarchical heterostructure improved the photoelectric conversion ef­ ficiency of the electrode (Fig. 4F). The IPCE values were calculated by Equ. (2) (Wang et al., 2020):
$$
I P C E=(\frac{1240J}{\lambda P_{l i g h t}})\ \times100\%
$$
where $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{light}}$ is the incident light power density, J is the photo-current density and $\lambda$ is the wavelength of the monochromatic light. The $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ has a wider visible light response compared to the single material, which due to the exclusive hollow heterostructure and energy level staggered matching n-n type II heterojunction.
To sum up all the above discussions on photoelectric enhancement mechanism, the most intuitive manifestation is the PEC capability of the $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ heterojunction electrode (Fig. 5A, curve b, $70.68~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ . There are the reasons for the superior PEC performance of $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}}S_{3}$ -HNRs: (ⅰ) The well-designed hollow nanocages and nanorods provide a large cavity for the multi-light scattering effect of light, thereby increasing the number of photoexcited carriers and improving the light absorption efficiency. (ⅱ) The $\mathrm{In}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{3}$ ultrathin nanosheet subunits of the surrounding the nanorods shell are favorable for quickly injecting charge carriers by reducing the diffusion length from the surface to the electrolyte (Wang et al., 2018). (ⅲ) The ultimate heterojunction with faultlessly matched band structure has high capa­ bility to inhibit photogenerated ${{\bf e}^{-}}/{{\bf h}^{+}}$ recombination and accelerate photogenerated electron-hole pairs of the separation under the visible light.
## 3.3. Fabrication of the PEC biosensor
The photoelectric performance and conversion efficiency are indis­ pensable indexes for the construction of biosensor electrodes. Based on the $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ double-hollow heterojunction electrode with remarkable performance in this work, a complete PEC biosensor for CA15-3 detection was constructed, and the mechanism and construction of sensors are exhibited in Scheme 1B. The relationship between photocurrent versus time was measured by the chopped light voltam­ metry (Fig. 5A). It was concluded that the single ZnCdS-HDCs (curve a, $27.76~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ with a wider band gap had the correspondingly weaker photocurrent, which were compared to the ZnCdS-HDCs@In2S3-HNRs double-hollow heterojunction. Then, with the aldimine condensation reaction of CS and GA on the ITO electrode surface, the photocurrent of the modified heterojunction electrode was decreased significantly (curve c, $58.37~\upmu\mathrm{A})$ . The photocurrent intensity was further declined after the anti-CA15-3 subsequently was coated on ITO/ZnCdS$\mathrm{\mathrm{HDC}}s@\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs/CS/GA electrode films (curve d, $49.18\upmu\mathrm{A})$ . The BSA solution was applied to remove the inactive site (curve e, $42.40\upmu\mathrm{A}\mathrm{)}$ and finally the CA15-3 was dropped onto the electrode, confirming the success of the nonspecific binding from curve f.
To characterize the interface properties of the electrode, EIS was introduced with $0.1\mathrm{mM}$ KCl solution including 5.0 mM $\mathsf{[F e(C N)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ as a redox probe. As shown in Fig. 5B, the Nyquist plots of the ZnCdS$\mathrm{\mathrm{HDC}}s@\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}$ -HNRs heterojunction was exposed the optimal $\mathrm{\bfR}_{\mathrm{et}}$ value (curve a, $27.56\Omega\mathrm{cm}^{2};$ ), which was benefited from stagger band structure to accelerate photogenerated electron-hole pairs of the separation. However, with the modification of the GA, CA15-3 antibody, BSA and CA15-3 the semicircle of the electron transfer resistance value increased step by step (curve c-f), which is due to the increase of electron transfer obstruction in the modified electrodes. The above experimental results successfully confirmed that the stable binding of each modification unit in the PEC biosensor.
## 3.4. Stability, sensitive and the parameter optimization of PEC biosensor
To develop a PEC biosensor with the exceedingly nice performance, the important parameters were optimized in the sensing electrode con­ struction process (see the Supporting Information for details). In Fig. 5C, the photocurrent variation was inversely proportional to the logarithmic value of the concentration of CA15-3. he regression equation was $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{~I~}}=$ $-5.21311\mathrm{g}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{CA}15-3}+14.6455$ with the correlation coefficient of 0.992 and the limit of detection (LOD) is $3.78\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{U{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ $(S/\Nu=3)$ , ranging from $1.0\times10^{-5}$ to $10\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . The photoelectrochemical sensor in this paper was emerged a subtler response to the detection of CA15-3 in Table S1, which compares the performance for the detection of CA15- 3 in previous work, the PEC biological detection exhibits the wider detection range and lower detection limit.
To research the reproducibility of the PEC biosensor, the seven identical PEC biosensor electrodes ${\bf\left(0.01U{\cdot}m L^{-1}\right)}$ were prepared for the detection of CA 15-3. From Fig. 5D, the RSD between different electrode was $2.59\%$ indicating that the constructed PEC biosensor possessed excellent reproducibility. Afterward, the transient photocurrent response of the potential interfering proteins such as PSA; AFP; CEA; BSA; the carcinoma antigen 19-9 (CA19-9) and a mixture containing all the mentioned independent interferences were explored the specificity of the PEC biosensors. In contrast with the restrained photocurrent in the CA15-3 sample $(10\ \mathrm{U{\cdotmL}^{-1}})$ , the photocurrent of the biosensor incu­ bated with interferometer not change significantly, which indicated that the biosensor was endowed outstanding selectivity (Fig. 5E). Finally, we successfully demonstrated the excellent stability of the incubated 0.1 $\mathbf{U}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L}^{-1}$ biosensor electrode. Recording the photocurrent response of the PEC biosensor confirmed that the stability of the electrode was within the acceptable range during 20 consecutive on-off irradiation cycles for 400 s (Fig. 5F). The above measurements were benefited from the excellent photoelectric conversion performance of the ZnCdS$\mathrm{\mathrm{HDCs@In_{2}S_{3}}}$ -HNRs double-hollow heterojunction biosensor, which had a reliable performance of detecting CA15-3.
## 3.5. Real sample analysis
To evaluate the applicability of the proposed PEC biosensor in the actual situation, three different concentrations (0.01, 0.1 and $1\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) of CA15-3 were applied with human serum samples. The human serum sample diluted 10-fold with PBS (0.1 M, $\mathfrak{p H}=7.4\,$ (Huang et al., 2017) to alleviate the interference of complex matrix, and following spiked with CA15-3 antigen for evaluating the applicability of the fabricated biosensor via standard addition recovery method. The performance of the well-constructed biosensor electrode in real samples was evaluated with human serum samples with known concentration of CA15-3 under optimal experimental conditions. As displayed in Table 1, the RSD was $0.92\%-3.57\%$ , and the recovery rates were in the range of $97.5\%-$ $104.5\%$ , revealing that the designed PEC biosensor showed adequate applicability in serum samples.
## 4. Conclusions
To summarize, the $\mathrm{In}_{2}S_{3}–\mathrm{HNRs}$ with ultra-thin nanosheets were successfully synthesized by self-template solvothermal method. Furthermore, we have elaborately constituted the $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}}$ HNRs double-hollow heterostructure by staggered matched energy level structure, which tremendously improved the photoelectrochemical performance of heterojunction electrode. Importantly, the late-model heterojunction of the double-hollow material significantly enhanced the charge separation efficiency and visible light capture capability. Simultaneously, the ultrathin nanosheet subunits of the surrounding the shell were conducive for quickly injecting charge carriers. The photo­ electric conversion performance was significantly promoted due to the structure and composition characteristics of the original heterojunction. Therefore, based on the high-efficiency photoelectric conversion in the $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s},$ the PEC biosensors exhibited splendid the precision, anti-interference ability and reproductivity. Simultaneously, the well-constituted biosensor manifested a wide linear range of $1.0\times$ $10^{-5}$ to $10~\mathrm{U}{\cdot}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ , and achieved a low detection limit $(3.78\times10^{-6}$ $\mathbf{U}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{L}^{-1}.$ ) to CA15-3. This work not only presents the extraordinary po­ tential of hollow photoactive the $\mathrm{ZnCdS\mathrm{-}H D C s@I n_{2}S_{3}\mathrm{-}H N R s}$ doublehollow heterojunction in PEC sensing applications, but also offers an advanced biosensor material platform for bioanalysis, disease detection and cancer treatment.
